{
  // **One formatter per language, and named.** Four things here can format: ESLint (the default below, which
  // runs Prettier through `eslint-plugin-prettier`), the Prettier extension, the Astro extension, and the
  // formatters built into VS Code. Naming a default is not enough on its own — when the default provider
  // declines a file, VS Code silently falls back to the next one, which is how one file ends up with two
  // different shapes depending on which extension happened to win.
  "editor.defaultFormatter": "dbaeumer.vscode-eslint",
  "editor.formatOnPaste": true,
  "editor.formatOnSave": true,
  // A third writer, firing while you type on a file the save action formats anyway, and the setting most
  // likely to be experienced as "the formatter keeps fighting me".
  "editor.formatOnType": false,
  // `detectIndentation` beats `tabSize`: one file with four-space indentation makes VS Code use four for that
  // file, Prettier then rewrites it to two, and the diff reads as a formatter that changed its mind.
  "editor.detectIndentation": false,
  "editor.tabSize": 2,
  // The built-in TypeScript/JavaScript/JSON formatters are the fallback that makes a collision invisible:
  // ESLint declines nothing, but when it errors the built-in takes over and writes a *different* shape.
  "js/ts.format.enabled": false,
  "json.format.enable": false,
  // ESLint cannot parse `.astro`, so it is named. It goes to Prettier itself rather than to the Astro
  // extension's bundled copy, because the workspace's `prettier.config.mjs` declares
  // `prettier-plugin-astro` — and a bundled copy resolves its own plugin set, which is the divergence the
  // project config exists to remove. Naming it is the whole of it: `editor.defaultFormatter` decides
  // who writes, so no extension has to be switched off for another to be trusted. (The setting the
  // settings UI does not offer inside a language block is `prettier.enable` — it is declared
  // `"scope": "resource"` in the extension's own manifest, exactly the scope a language block is, so a
  // valid override; being absent from the UI's picker is not the same as being invalid here.)
  "[astro]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  // JSON is the case that shows why a named default is not enough on its own. The lint path applies Prettier
  // to `js`, `mjs`, `cjs` and `ts` and **not** to JSON — `EXT.JSON` is declared in `eslint.config.mts` and
  // never used in a `files:` list — so with the built-in formatter off, JSON had no formatter at all: ESLint
  // declines it, the built-in is disabled, and the file is left silently as it was. Measured on a fixture:
  // `eslint --fix` leaves a messy JSON file untouched, `prettier --write` does not.
  "[json]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  "[jsonc]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  }, // CSS, HTML, Markdown and YAML are the same case as JSON: ESLint either ignores them (`**/*.css`
  // is in its ignores, and nothing registers a parser for YAML) or cannot parse them (HTML, which it
  // treats as JavaScript), so with the built-ins off they had no formatter at all. Every one of them
  // is Prettier's to own, which is also what `prettier --check .` now asserts across the repository.
  "[css]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  "[html]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  "[markdown]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  "[yaml]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "esbenp.prettier-vscode"
  },
  "css.format.enable": false,
  "html.format.enable": false,
  // **Prettier is enabled here, and does not need to be switched off to be kept out of code files.**
  //
  // This file used to disable it globally and re-enable it per language. Removing those re-enables —
  // which are valid, whatever the UI suggests — left the disable in force, so Prettier was off in the
  // seven languages that name it above and had no formatter at all. Nothing was gained by the switch:
  // `editor.defaultFormatter` already decides who writes, and a provider that is never *selected*
  // cannot format anything however enabled it is. The only thing keeping Prettier out of `.ts` is that
  // `.ts` does not name it.
  // **The TypeScript language service writes code here, not just diagnoses it.**
  //
  // `codeActionsOnSave` below asks it to fix all (`source.fixAll.ts`), add missing imports and remove
  // unused ones. Two of those rewrite the import block, and the service prints the statements it
  // *synthesizes* with semicolons: the default `format.semicolons: ignore` preserves what a file
  // already has, but it says nothing about text the service generates itself. So ESLint's fix-all takes
  // the semicolons off, the service puts them back on the import lines it rewrote, and the file is
  // left half-formatted with the disagreement visible on two lines and nowhere else.
  //
  // `remove` is the setting for a project that does not use them, and it governs both paths.
  "js/ts.format.semicolons": "remove",
  // **Biome is not installed here, and this line is a guard rather than a fix.**
  //
  // The directory `biomejs.biome-3.6.1` exists, which is not the same as being installed:
  // `extensions.json` — the authoritative list — does not contain it, `.obsolete` lists five older
  // versions, and 3.6.1 belongs to `~/.cursor/extensions`. An extension directory outlives its
  // extension, so "it is on disk" is not evidence that it can write. It is kept because there is no
  // `biome.json` in this repository and Biome's default (`semi: true`) disagrees with
  // `prettier.config.mjs`, so if it is ever installed and enabled again it would reintroduce exactly
  // the two-formatter fight this file exists to prevent.
  "biome.enabled": false,
  // And the four languages ESLint owns are named explicitly, because a *language-specific* default
  // beats this file's language-agnostic one. A user-level `[typescript]` block names Prettier, so
  // without these four lines the formatter for `.ts` would be Prettier — which is enabled, and has to
  // be, for JSON and CSS above. Naming ESLint here is what keeps that enablement from reaching code.
  "[javascript]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "dbaeumer.vscode-eslint"
  },
  "[typescript]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "dbaeumer.vscode-eslint"
  },
  "[javascriptreact]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "dbaeumer.vscode-eslint"
  },
  "[typescriptreact]": {
    "editor.defaultFormatter": "dbaeumer.vscode-eslint"
  },
  "editor.codeActionsOnSave": {
    "source.addMissingImports.ts": "always",
    "source.fixAll.eslint": "always",
    "source.fixAll.ts": "always",
    "source.removeUnusedImports": "always"
  },
  "css.customData": [".vscode/tailwind.v4.json"],
  // The Tailwind IntelliSense extension builds one project per CSS file that imports "tailwindcss",
  // and creating them all is what exhausted its heap. Six files here are real entry points: the four under
  // docs/src/styles/ and docs/src/details/, stories/globals.css, and examples/input.css. The `scripts/`
  // corpora are harness fixtures, and storybook-static/ and docs/.astro/ are build output; none of them
  // should be built as a project. Overriding this setting replaces the extension's defaults
  // (**/.git/**, **/node_modules/**, **/.hg/**, **/.svn/**), so they are repeated here.
  //
  // Excluding paths only caps the number of *projects*. Their cost is what each entry scans, which is why
  // every entry declares its sources (`source(none)` plus `@source`) instead of a bare
  // `@import 'tailwindcss'` that scans the workspace. That was worth 1.2 GB and 200 MB of CSS in
  // stories/globals.css alone, and it crashed the server five times on 2026-09-15.
  //
  // Scoping the scan was not the end of it. Measured on 2026-09-23, per entry, peak RSS and emitted bytes:
  //
  //   examples/input.css              136 MB    185 KB
  //   stories/globals.css             379 MB     49 MB      <-- the outlier
  //   docs/src/styles/motion.css      135 MB    4.6 KB
  //   docs/src/styles/catalog.css     136 MB    8.8 KB
  //   docs/src/styles/demo.css        138 MB    5.8 KB
  //   docs/src/details/zetta-joule.css 135 MB   18.2 KB
  //
  // The stories entry is not scanning anything it should not: those 49 MB are 4,182 rules at roughly
  // 12 KB each, because every staged rule carries the eight-part `--jumi-<property>-animation-<part>`
  // fallback chain, and that chain lengthens with every property family the library grows. Five such
  // projects, rebuilt in watch mode every time `pnpm check` rewrites the plugin it watches, is what
  // crashes a ~3.2 GB Electron heap; the last line of the extension log before each OOM was the watch
  // pattern for `dist/index.js`.
  //
  // So the projects are declared explicitly, and the outlier is left out. Everything else keeps working,
  // including the docs pages that depend on their entry. What is lost is completion inside
  // `stories/**`, which is a dev surface rather than a shipping one; Storybook still builds that entry
  // from `.storybook/main.ts`, which this setting does not touch. Add it back here if completions there
  // matter more than a server that stays up.
  "tailwindCSS.experimental.configFile": {
    "docs/src/pages/index.astro": "docs/src/styles/motion.css",
    "docs/src/pages/effects.astro": "docs/src/styles/catalog.css",
    "docs/src/data/effects.json": "docs/src/styles/catalog.css",
    "docs/src/pages/demo/**": "docs/src/styles/demo.css",
    "docs/src/pages/details/**": "docs/src/details/zetta-joule.css",
    "examples/**": "examples/input.css"
  },
  "tailwindCSS.files.exclude": [
    "**/.git/**",
    "**/node_modules/**",
    "**/.hg/**",
    "**/.svn/**",
    "**/scripts/**",
    "**/storybook-static/**",
    "**/docs/.astro/**",
    "**/.pnpm-store/**"
  ]
}
